Subject: lv: draw the FPS readout as a whole number

Upstream's Show FPS draws "59.83" — two decimals of a one-second average, in
the N64 numeric font, in the top-left corner of a phone screen. The user, on
device (2026-09-14): "the fps shows 2 decimal points. whole numbers only."

Two decimals are noise at any refresh rate a panel can show, and on a 390-point
screen the extra three glyphs are a third of the readout's width. The value
itself is unchanged (videoGetAverageFPS() still averages over
Video.DisplayFPSInterval); only the format is.

Not PLATFORM_IOS-guarded: a whole number is the right readout everywhere, and
this is on the list to offer upstream (Q-002).

diff --git a/src/game/lv.c b/src/game/lv.c
index 2bc30ed46..6c1591263 100644
--- a/src/game/lv.c
+++ b/src/game/lv.c
@@ -1128,7 +1128,7 @@ Gfx *lvRenderFPS(Gfx *gdl)
 	}
 
 	if (g_CharsNumeric && g_FontNumeric) {
-		snprintf(buffer, sizeof buffer, "%.2f", fps);
+		snprintf(buffer, sizeof buffer, "%.0f", fps);
 
 		gSPSetExtraGeometryModeEXT(gdl++, g_HudAlignModeL);
 
